1
00:00:00,240 --> 00:00:02,800
On opening day, London's new footbridge

2
00:00:02,800 --> 00:00:04,560
began swaying sideways.

3
00:00:04,560 --> 00:00:06,080
People grabbed the railings.

4
00:00:06,080 --> 00:00:08,400
Two days later, the bridge closed.

5
00:00:08,400 --> 00:00:10,000
Engineers had two questions.

6
00:00:10,160 --> 00:00:11,920
How does a bridge like this move?

7
00:00:11,920 --> 00:00:14,880
And why did a crowd make that motion grow?

8
00:00:14,880 --> 00:00:16,880
The first question takes a little linear

9
00:00:16,880 --> 00:00:18,080
algebra.

10
00:00:18,080 --> 00:00:20,320
This is the Millennium Bridge, London's

11
00:00:20,320 --> 00:00:22,000
showpiece for the new millennium.

12
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:24,400
It's a sleek steel suspension bridge, with

13
00:00:24,400 --> 00:00:26,320
a main span of one hundred forty four

14
00:00:26,320 --> 00:00:28,480
meters. It opened on the tenth of June,

15
00:00:28,480 --> 00:00:29,200
two thousand.

16
00:00:29,360 --> 00:00:31,360
An estimated eighty to one hundred

17
00:00:31,360 --> 00:00:33,440
thousand people crossed that first day,

18
00:00:33,440 --> 00:00:35,040
with about two thousand on the bridge at

19
00:00:35,040 --> 00:00:36,240
the busiest moments.

20
00:00:36,240 --> 00:00:38,720
Soon the deck was moving sideways.

21
00:00:38,720 --> 00:00:40,800
Crowd numbers were limited, and on Sunday

22
00:00:40,880 --> 00:00:42,320
the sway came less often.

23
00:00:42,320 --> 00:00:43,840
But it kept coming back.

24
00:00:43,840 --> 00:00:45,760
On the twelfth of June, just two days

25
00:00:45,760 --> 00:00:48,240
after opening, the bridge was closed.

26
00:00:48,240 --> 00:00:49,600
How bad was it?

27
00:00:49,600 --> 00:00:51,600
Engineers later estimated the motion from

28
00:00:51,600 --> 00:00:53,440
what people saw and from video.

29
00:00:53,440 --> 00:00:55,680
The centre span was swinging sideways by

30
00:00:55,680 --> 00:00:57,520
about seven centimeters, with peak

31
00:00:57,600 --> 00:00:59,520
sideways accelerations of up to about a

32
00:00:59,520 --> 00:01:00,720
quarter of g.

33
00:01:00,910 --> 00:01:02,270
People were struggling to keep their

34
00:01:02,270 --> 00:01:04,430
balance. Many stopped walking, and some

35
00:01:04,430 --> 00:01:06,830
grabbed the railings just to stay upright.

36
00:01:06,830 --> 00:01:09,230
To engineers, that motion raises two

37
00:01:09,230 --> 00:01:11,310
questions. First, how does this bridge

38
00:01:11,310 --> 00:01:12,190
tend to move?

39
00:01:12,190 --> 00:01:13,870
That's about its natural frequencies and

40
00:01:13,870 --> 00:01:16,350
mode shapes. Second, why did the motion

41
00:01:16,350 --> 00:01:18,190
grow instead of dying out?

42
00:01:18,190 --> 00:01:19,550
That's about energy.

43
00:01:19,550 --> 00:01:21,230
Let's start with the first, using the

44
00:01:21,230 --> 00:01:22,990
simplest structure there is.

45
00:01:22,990 --> 00:01:25,470
Picture a single mass on a spring.

46
00:01:25,470 --> 00:01:27,470
Pull it sideways and let go.

47
00:01:27,470 --> 00:01:29,950
It swings back and forth at one particular

48
00:01:29,950 --> 00:01:31,710
rate, all by itself.

49
00:01:31,710 --> 00:01:34,030
That rate is its natural frequency.

50
00:01:34,190 --> 00:01:36,430
It depends on two things: how stiff the

51
00:01:36,430 --> 00:01:39,310
spring is, k, and how much mass is moving,

52
00:01:39,310 --> 00:01:42,670
m. The natural frequency f equals one over

53
00:01:42,670 --> 00:01:45,950
two pi, times the square root of k over m.

54
00:01:45,950 --> 00:01:47,390
Stiffer means faster.

55
00:01:47,390 --> 00:01:48,990
More mass means slower.

56
00:01:48,990 --> 00:01:51,150
A real bridge works on the same idea,

57
00:01:51,150 --> 00:01:53,150
except its stiffness and mass are spread

58
00:01:53,150 --> 00:01:55,070
throughout the whole structure.

59
00:01:55,070 --> 00:01:58,270
Now take two identical masses, joined by

60
00:01:58,270 --> 00:02:00,830
three identical springs, wall to wall.

61
00:02:00,900 --> 00:02:02,580
We'll ignore damping for now.

62
00:02:02,580 --> 00:02:04,820
Write Newton's law for each mass and you

63
00:02:04,820 --> 00:02:07,060
get two equations tangled together.

64
00:02:07,060 --> 00:02:09,860
Linear algebra packs them into one line.

65
00:02:09,860 --> 00:02:12,820
K is the stiffness matrix, M is the mass

66
00:02:12,820 --> 00:02:15,540
matrix, and v lists how far each mass

67
00:02:15,540 --> 00:02:17,460
moves. We look for motions where

68
00:02:17,460 --> 00:02:19,860
everything swings at one shared angular

69
00:02:19,860 --> 00:02:21,460
frequency, omega.

70
00:02:21,460 --> 00:02:25,220
That gives K v equals omega squared M v.

71
00:02:25,220 --> 00:02:26,740
It's an eigenvalue problem.

72
00:02:26,740 --> 00:02:29,060
To turn omega into hertz, divide by two

73
00:02:29,060 --> 00:02:29,690
pi.

74
00:02:29,700 --> 00:02:30,820
Let's solve it.

75
00:02:30,820 --> 00:02:33,700
Divide both sides by k, and call m omega

76
00:02:33,860 --> 00:02:35,860
squared over k, lambda.

77
00:02:35,860 --> 00:02:37,780
That leaves a matrix with twos on the

78
00:02:37,780 --> 00:02:40,180
diagonal and minus ones off it.

79
00:02:40,180 --> 00:02:42,500
An eigenvalue is a lambda that makes this

80
00:02:42,500 --> 00:02:43,940
determinant zero.

81
00:02:43,940 --> 00:02:46,180
That gives two minus lambda, squared,

82
00:02:46,340 --> 00:02:48,100
minus one, equals zero.

83
00:02:48,100 --> 00:02:50,580
So lambda is one or three.

84
00:02:50,580 --> 00:02:52,740
For this example, let's choose the springs

85
00:02:52,740 --> 00:02:54,740
and masses so the first frequency is one

86
00:02:54,740 --> 00:02:57,060
hertz. Then the second is root three times

87
00:02:57,060 --> 00:02:59,460
higher, about one point seven three hertz.

88
00:02:59,890 --> 00:03:02,770
Each eigenvalue comes with an eigenvector,

89
00:03:02,770 --> 00:03:04,130
called a mode shape.

90
00:03:04,130 --> 00:03:06,130
In the first mode, both masses move

91
00:03:06,130 --> 00:03:08,290
together. The middle spring stays the same

92
00:03:08,290 --> 00:03:10,610
length. That's the one hertz mode.

93
00:03:10,610 --> 00:03:12,370
In the second, they move in opposite

94
00:03:12,370 --> 00:03:14,050
directions. Now the middle spring

95
00:03:14,050 --> 00:03:16,130
stretches and compresses too, so that mode

96
00:03:16,130 --> 00:03:17,660
has a higher frequency, about one point

97
00:03:17,670 --> 00:03:19,730
seven three hertz.

98
00:03:19,730 --> 00:03:21,970
In this model, any free vibration can be

99
00:03:21,970 --> 00:03:23,730
described as a combination of these two

100
00:03:23,730 --> 00:03:24,610
modes.

101
00:03:24,610 --> 00:03:26,850
A real bridge works the same way, only

102
00:03:26,930 --> 00:03:29,570
bigger. Engineers build a finite element

103
00:03:29,650 --> 00:03:31,730
model, a computer model of how mass and

104
00:03:31,730 --> 00:03:33,090
stiffness are spread through the

105
00:03:33,090 --> 00:03:34,930
structure. That gives matrices with

106
00:03:34,930 --> 00:03:36,050
thousands of rows.

107
00:03:36,050 --> 00:03:37,880
Their eigenvalues are squared angular

108
00:03:37,890 --> 00:03:40,530
frequencies. Their eigenvectors describe

109
00:03:40,530 --> 00:03:42,610
the mode shapes: bending up and down,

110
00:03:42,770 --> 00:03:44,850
twisting, or swaying sideways.

111
00:03:44,850 --> 00:03:46,770
For a footbridge, the low modes are the

112
00:03:46,770 --> 00:03:48,370
ones to watch, because they're close to

113
00:03:48,370 --> 00:03:49,810
the rhythm of walking.

114
00:03:49,810 --> 00:03:51,650
So what's the rhythm of walking?

115
00:03:51,810 --> 00:03:53,890
Most people take about two steps every

116
00:03:53,890 --> 00:03:56,370
second. Each step pushes down, so the main

117
00:03:56,370 --> 00:03:57,970
up and down force comes at about two

118
00:03:58,130 --> 00:04:00,160
hertz. Now watch from above.

119
00:04:00,160 --> 00:04:02,000
You shift your weight onto your left foot,

120
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:03,120
then your right foot.

121
00:04:03,120 --> 00:04:06,000
One full left right cycle takes two steps.

122
00:04:06,000 --> 00:04:07,840
So the sideways force comes at about one

123
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:10,320
hertz. That's half your step rate.

124
00:04:10,320 --> 00:04:11,760
Now look at the bridge.

125
00:04:11,760 --> 00:04:13,430
The south span swayed at about point seven

126
00:04:13,440 --> 00:04:17,120
seven hertz. The centre span swayed near

127
00:04:17,120 --> 00:04:19,200
point nine five hertz, close to that one

128
00:04:19,360 --> 00:04:20,960
hertz walking rhythm.

129
00:04:20,960 --> 00:04:23,520
So far, that sounds like simple resonance.

130
00:04:23,520 --> 00:04:25,920
But the centre span also swayed at just

131
00:04:25,920 --> 00:04:27,520
under point five hertz.

132
00:04:27,520 --> 00:04:29,440
That's about half the walking rhythm, far

133
00:04:29,600 --> 00:04:30,560
from a match.

134
00:04:30,560 --> 00:04:32,640
So comparing walking with one bridge

135
00:04:32,640 --> 00:04:34,800
frequency can't be the whole story.

136
00:04:34,800 --> 00:04:36,880
Here's what resonance looks like when a

137
00:04:36,880 --> 00:04:38,880
steady outside force pushes on a

138
00:04:38,880 --> 00:04:40,960
structure. Think of pushing a child on a

139
00:04:40,960 --> 00:04:43,920
swing. Small pushes, in time, build a big

140
00:04:43,920 --> 00:04:46,160
motion. This example structure has a

141
00:04:46,160 --> 00:04:48,000
natural frequency of one hertz.

142
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:50,320
The graph compares its motion with how far

143
00:04:50,320 --> 00:04:52,240
the same force would move it if you pushed

144
00:04:52,240 --> 00:04:54,720
very slowly. At very low frequencies, the

145
00:04:54,720 --> 00:04:56,240
ratio is about one.

146
00:04:56,330 --> 00:04:58,410
At one hertz, with five percent damping,

147
00:04:58,490 --> 00:04:59,690
it's ten times.

148
00:04:59,690 --> 00:05:01,530
With twenty percent damping, it's two and

149
00:05:01,610 --> 00:05:02,490
a half times.

150
00:05:02,490 --> 00:05:04,330
Damping keeps the peak down.

151
00:05:04,330 --> 00:05:07,210
But a crowd isn't a steady outside force.

152
00:05:07,210 --> 00:05:09,690
A moving bridge changes how people push on

153
00:05:09,690 --> 00:05:10,240
it.

154
00:05:10,250 --> 00:05:12,250
For years, the popular explanation was

155
00:05:12,250 --> 00:05:14,090
that walkers fell into step with each

156
00:05:14,090 --> 00:05:16,090
other. Newer research shows they don't

157
00:05:16,090 --> 00:05:18,730
have to. On this bridge, the deck itself

158
00:05:18,730 --> 00:05:20,250
changed how people walked.

159
00:05:20,250 --> 00:05:22,010
As it shifted under their feet, they

160
00:05:22,010 --> 00:05:24,170
adjusted their steps to stay balanced.

161
00:05:24,170 --> 00:05:26,490
On average, those adjustments fed energy

162
00:05:26,490 --> 00:05:28,650
back into the sideways motion, even when

163
00:05:28,650 --> 00:05:30,410
people were walking out of step.

164
00:05:30,410 --> 00:05:32,810
So think of each sway as a balance.

165
00:05:32,810 --> 00:05:34,330
Walkers add energy.

166
00:05:34,330 --> 00:05:36,170
Damping takes energy away.

167
00:05:36,170 --> 00:05:38,810
If damping wins, the sway fades.

168
00:05:38,810 --> 00:05:41,290
If the walkers win, the sway grows.

169
00:05:41,290 --> 00:05:43,370
Engineers saw this tipping point in a

170
00:05:43,370 --> 00:05:45,850
test. After the closure, they gradually

171
00:05:45,850 --> 00:05:47,850
added walkers to one span.

172
00:05:47,850 --> 00:05:49,610
With around one hundred sixty five people

173
00:05:49,610 --> 00:05:51,930
on the test span, the sway began to grow

174
00:05:51,930 --> 00:05:54,520
sharply. Past that point, the crowd was

175
00:05:54,520 --> 00:05:56,440
putting energy into the motion faster than

176
00:05:56,440 --> 00:05:57,720
the bridge could lose it.

177
00:05:57,960 --> 00:06:00,120
Making the bridge much stiffer would have

178
00:06:00,120 --> 00:06:01,720
changed its look completely.

179
00:06:01,720 --> 00:06:04,680
So instead, engineers added damping, to

180
00:06:04,680 --> 00:06:06,120
take energy out.

181
00:06:06,120 --> 00:06:08,520
They fitted thirty seven fluid viscous

182
00:06:08,520 --> 00:06:10,680
dampers, a lot like the shock absorbers in

183
00:06:10,680 --> 00:06:12,920
a car, mainly for the sideways motion.

184
00:06:12,920 --> 00:06:15,160
They also installed fifty two tuned mass

185
00:06:15,160 --> 00:06:17,080
dampers for the up and down motion.

186
00:06:17,080 --> 00:06:19,640
The work cost about five million pounds.

187
00:06:19,640 --> 00:06:21,720
In tests of the modified bridge, with

188
00:06:21,720 --> 00:06:23,560
about two thousand people, the motion

189
00:06:23,560 --> 00:06:25,560
stayed at acceptable levels.

190
00:06:25,800 --> 00:06:27,363
On the twenty second of February, two

191
00:06:27,373 --> 00:06:29,560
thousand two, the Millennium Bridge

192
00:06:29,560 --> 00:06:32,280
reopened. The dampers sit under and beside

193
00:06:32,280 --> 00:06:34,280
the deck, mostly out of sight.

194
00:06:34,280 --> 00:06:36,840
The bridge kept its light, slender shape,

195
00:06:36,840 --> 00:06:39,240
and its natural frequencies still describe

196
00:06:39,240 --> 00:06:40,600
how it can move.

197
00:06:40,600 --> 00:06:42,520
What changed is the balance.

198
00:06:42,520 --> 00:06:44,840
The dampers now take energy out faster

199
00:06:44,840 --> 00:06:47,000
than a normal crowd puts it in.

200
00:06:47,000 --> 00:06:49,080
The same two questions come up wherever

201
00:06:49,080 --> 00:06:50,600
crowds meet structures.

202
00:06:50,600 --> 00:06:52,280
Take stadium stands.

203
00:06:52,280 --> 00:06:54,120
Engineers check their natural frequencies,

204
00:06:54,200 --> 00:06:56,280
so a crowd jumping to music doesn't line

205
00:06:56,280 --> 00:06:56,980
up with them.

206
00:06:57,150 --> 00:06:59,070
And they check there's enough damping for

207
00:06:59,070 --> 00:07:01,150
the energy a crowd can put in.

208
00:07:01,150 --> 00:07:02,430
So remember this.

209
00:07:02,430 --> 00:07:04,910
The eigenvalues told engineers how fast

210
00:07:04,910 --> 00:07:06,430
the bridge could sway.

211
00:07:06,430 --> 00:07:09,070
Whether that sway faded or grew depended

212
00:07:09,070 --> 00:07:11,150
on how much energy the crowd added and how

213
00:07:11,150 --> 00:07:12,750
much the bridge dissipated.

214
00:07:12,750 --> 00:07:15,150
The dampers tipped that balance.

215
00:07:15,150 --> 00:07:16,990
If you liked this video, give it a like

216
00:07:17,070 --> 00:07:18,750
and subscribe for more everyday

217
00:07:18,750 --> 00:07:20,670
engineering. Thanks for watching!
